  "text": "To find the value of \\( b \\) such that the polynomial \\( y^3 + 3y^2 + ay + b = 0 \\) has at least two distinct integer roots, we start by considering the roots of the polynomial \\( x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 = 0 \\). Let's denote the roots of this polynomial by \\( r_1, r_2, r_3 \\). According to Vieta's formulas, the sum of the roots taken one at a time is equal to the negation of the coefficient of \\( x^2 \\) divided by the coefficient of \\( x^3 \\), and so on. For the polynomial \\( x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 = 0 \\), we have:\n\n\\[ r_1 + r_2 + r_3 = -3. \\]\n\nNext, we consider the polynomial \\( y^3 + 3y^2 + ay + b = 0 \\). We want at least two distinct integer roots from the roots of the polynomial \\( x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 = 0 \\). Let's assume that \\( r_1 \\) and \\( r_2 \\) are the roots of the polynomial \\( y^3 + 3y^2 + ay + b = 0 \\). Therefore, \\( y = r_1 \\) and \\( y = r_2 \\) satisfy the equation \\( y^3 + 3y^2 + ay + b = 0 \\). Substituting \\( y = r_1 \\) and \\( y = r_2 \\) into the polynomial, we get:\n\n\\[ r_1^3 + 3r_1^2 + ar_1 + b = 0, \\]\n\\[ r_2^3 + 3r_2^2 + ar_2 + b = 0. \\]\n\nSubtracting these two equations, we obtain:\n\n\\[ r_1^3 - r_2^3 + 3r_1^2 - 3r_2^2 + ar_1 - ar_2 = 0. \\]\n\nThis can be factored as:\n\n\\[ (r_1 - r_2)(r_1^2 + r_1r_2 + r_2^2 - 3) + a(r_1 - r_2) = 0, \\]\n\\[ (r_1 - r_2)(r_1^2 + r_1r_2 + r_2^2 - 3 + a) = 0. \\]\n\nSince \\( r_1 \\neq r_2 \\), it must be that:\n\n\\[ r_1^2 + r_1r_2 + r_2^2 - 3 + a = 0, \\]\n\\[ a = 3 - (r_1^2 + r_1r_2 + r_2^2). \\]\n\nNow, we need to find the value of \\( b \\). Since \\( r_1 \\) and \\( r_2 \\) are roots of \\( y^3 + 3y^2 + ay + b = 0 \\), we substitute \\( y = r_1 \\) and \\( y = r_2 \\) into the polynomial and use the fact that \\( r_1 + r_2 + r_3 = -3 \\). From the polynomial \\( x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 = 0 \\), we know that \\( r_1 + r_2 + 7 = 0 \\) because the sum of the roots taken one at a time is \\(-3\\). Therefore, \\( r_1 + r_2 = -7 \\).\n\nSubstituting \\( y = r_1 \\) and \\( y = r_2 \\) into the polynomial \\( y^3 + 3y^2 + ay + b = 0 \\), we get:\n\n\\[ r_1(r_1 + r_2) + 7 = 0, \\]\n\\[ r_1(-7) + 7 = 0, \\]\n\\[ -7r_1 + 7 = 0, \\]\n\\[ r_1 = 1. \\]\n\nSimilarly, substituting \\( y = r_2 \\) into the polynomial \\( y^3 + 3y^2 + ay + b = 0 \\), we get:\n\n\\[ r_2(r_1 + r_2) + 7 = 0, \\]\n\\[ r_2(-7) + 7 = 0, \\]\n\\[ -7r_2 + 7 = 0, \\]\n\\[ r_2 = 1. \\]\n\nSince both roots are 1, there is only one distinct integer root, which means we need to find another root. Let's assume the third root is \\( r_3 = -7 \\) (since \\( r_1 + r_2 + r_3 = -3 \\)). Then, we can find \\( a \\) and \\( b \\):\n\n\\[ a = 3 - (r_1^2 + r_1r_2 + r_2^2) = 3 - (1^2 + 1 \\cdot 1 + 1^2) = 3 - 3 = 0. \\]\n\\[ b = 7 - (r_1^3 + 3r_1^2 + ar_1) = 7 - (1^3 + 3 \\cdot 1^2 + 0 \\cdot 1) = 7 - 4 = 3. \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\( b \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{33}\n\\]",
